Fictional EP Tracklist

Track 1: The Woolen Needle Sews the Interchange
- Story & Context: At 5:15 AM beside the Blackwall Tunnel approaches, Bramwell Croft pushed twenty meters of coarse Scottish sheep yarn into an open signal junction box, mechanically shorting the amber relays to trigger an unending sequence of hypnotic, rhythmic clicks that fed directly into his analog filters. The sudden gridlock halted three hundred morning milk trucks whose drivers smelled the rich steam of boiling chicory coming from Croft’s camp kettle before they heard the velvet four-on-the-floor thump creeping out of the roadside speakers. As Elspeth Vance stepped onto the roof of an oil tanker and laid a smoky, low-register saxophone motif across the idling V8 engines, she leaned down to the megaphone and sang, "We tie the grid in knots until the city breathes." Riot squads sent on foot to dismantle the apparatus found themselves paralyzed by the warmth of the sound and accepted paper cups of tea instead of clearing the tarmac.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Wide-angle cinematic album artwork, high grain, 1970s film style; an aerial view of a massive cloverleaf highway junction at dusk where the glowing lines of vehicle headlights form tangled skeins of amber yarn threaded by a giant, ethereal brass needle into foggy asphalt.

Track 2: "Who Snapped the Weft at Junction Seven?"
- Story & Context: Huddled beneath an oilskin tarp in the belly of the Salford flyover during an icy sleet storm, Elspeth Vance watched through tears as an over-volted transformer spat orange embers, singeing the sleeves of her mohair cardigan and instantly incinerating three reels of prerecorded guitar drones. Desperate to keep the hundreds of freezing motorists from restarting their engines, she clamped her lips to a frozen Selmer mouthpiece, coaxing hoarse, overdriven multiphonics while Croft frantically tapped an uninsulated live copper wire against a rusty road sign to keep the progressive house kick pulsing. Her hands shook violently from the cold and the fear of the approaching police convoy, yet her voice caught the tape echo with the heartbreaking hook: "Forgive my trembling hands, I only wanted to keep you warm." When the lights along the bypass finally failed, the crowd remained completely still in the dark, held suspended by the solitary decay of her saxophone pedal notes.
- Visual & Lyrics Prompt: Intimate, moody lyric video background, 1970s analog aesthetic; a weathered woman's hands in frayed fingerless wool gloves soldering wires inside a glowing metal switchbox while rain streaks across a cracked windshield, illuminated by warm, honey-colored dashboard lamps.

Track 3: Vance’s Braid on the Outer Ring
- Story & Context: Decades after the final winter roadblocks were cleared, veteran long-haul truckers still swear that on damp November nights near Junction Eleven of the Leeds periphery, the streetlights rhythmically shift into a warm, unmapped violet cadence. Those who switch off their CB radios report hearing an ancient, tape-warped Rhodes melody floating through the vents, joined by a ghost-kick that matches the thrum of road expansion joints and a distant tenor horn that seems to rise out of the drainage culverts. Road maintenance workers frequently dig up strange lengths of yarn intertwined with decayed copper conductors inside the roadside cable troughs, releasing a curious, preserved aroma of pipe tobacco and wet cedar shavings into the cold night. Local mythology insists that Vance and Croft never left the ring road at all, leaving a subterranean post-rock crescendo looping beneath the foundations to remind the night drivers: "The asphalt unspools, but our stitches will hold."
